#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218180} :activity/id #uuid "6025c8e1-49e2-5df5-8df4-3dade38539ab" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048704 :topic/id #uuid "b6d24642-a555-52bc-b8a8-d502351c1ca3" :topic/math-academy-id 6364} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218180 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218354 :tutorial/content "Gravitation is the attractive force that acts between *any two objects with mass*. This means that every pair of objects in the universe is pulling on each other, even if the effect is too small to notice in everyday life.\n\nIn many situations (especially when objects are far apart compared to their sizes), we model each object as if *all of its mass were concentrated at its center of mass*.\nThen the gravitational force depends only on the two masses and the distance between their centers of mass.\n\n![](images/dd/dd462f94b3a49b33ff44cf0868975f2ae902145a9664ce307a397fcc2a2ea93a.png)\n\n**Newton's law of universal gravitation** states that the gravitational force attracting two objects with masses is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons ($\\textrm{N}$),\n\n- $G \\approx 6.674\\times10^{-11}\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian **constant of gravitation**,\n\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nThe force acts along the line connecting the two centers of mass, and each object pulls on the other with the same magnitude.\n\nNewton's law shows two key relationships:\n\n- *More mass means more force:* If either mass increases, the gravitational force increases proportionally.\n\n- *Greater distance means less force:* The force decreases with the square of the distance. So, for example, doubling the distance makes the force one quarter as large.\n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "135ab76f-4555-55f3-97ed-0c3e76990bc8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14552 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "830fbba9-b0a4-5f52-82dc-b497af4a9670" :step/math-academy-id 587393 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218181}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218181 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217235 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216999 :question/id #uuid "f37e7ffd-df2d-51a4-9eee-e53589011f6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21744"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61c3724b-7fda-5547-b0cb-7bf9136fc4a3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049890}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496326}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gravitational Force Between Two Objects"} :step/id #uuid "d1d1c724-94a7-5a92-995f-3894e6b8d209" :step/math-academy-id 587395 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218182}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218182 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217236 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217000 :question/id #uuid "2ef6ba7e-4f1d-51d2-af64-e1a77a7d748f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21745"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd5da040-4fa3-5aeb-a98f-2a2ba03f5e16" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048274}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311678} {:db/id #edb/ref 1